   Jin, Yihan and Kamal, Waqas and Elston, Steve J. and Morris, Stephen M.},
Title = {Locking Metastable Topological Domains in Nematic Liquid Crystal Pi
   Cells},
Journal = {ADVANCED FUNCTIONAL MATERIALS},
Year = {2025},
Volume = {35},
Number = {51},
Month = {DEC},
Abstract = {The problem of stabilizing and switching between transient states in a
   Pi cell has long challenged the liquid crystal display industry. Here,
   distinct topological domain regions within nematic liquid crystal (LC)
   pi-cells are manipulated by controlling the nucleation and growth
   processes in ways that indefinitely stabilize LC states that are
   traditionally only observed as being metastable. Photo-polymerization
   under controlled voltage imprints patterns corresponding to the bend and
   splay states and impedes nucleation across topological regimes, ensuring
   long-term maintenance of complex director configurations. This
   methodology results in a high-speed Pi cell shutter, enabling rapid
   transitions between topologically compatible bend and twisted states
   while completely suppressing nucleation into the topologically
   incompatible splay state. Moreover, bistability is established through a
   combination of thermal and voltage control, thereby enabling a range of
   practical applications that capitalize on these stable configurations.
   Q-tensor simulations corroborate the experiments, demonstrating the
   temporal and spatial dynamics of nucleation and topological state
   stabilization. By precisely controlling the locking of director states
   across varying topologies, a bistable LC device is constructed with a
   voltage-driven hysteresis loop. These findings represent a significant
   advancement in the manipulation of LC device topologies, opening the
   door to advanced functionalities and broader applications for Pi cells.},
